{title:Title}

{phang}
{bf:ccollapse} {hline 2} C-accelerated collapse: make a dataset of summary statistics


{marker syntax}{...}
{title:Syntax}

{p 8 17 2}
{cmdab:ccollapse}
{it:clist}
{ifin}
{weight}
[{cmd:,} {it:options}]

{pstd}
where {it:clist} is either

{p 8 17 2}
[{opt (stat)}]
{varlist}
[ [{opt (stat)}] {it:...} ]{p_end}

{p 8 17 2}
[{opt (stat)}] {it:target_var}{cmd:=}{varname}
[{it:target_var}{cmd:=}{varname} {it:...}]
[ [{opt (stat)}] {it:...}]

{pstd}
or any combination of the {it:varlist} and {it:target_var} forms, and {it:stat}
is one of

{p2colset 9 22 24 2}{...}
{p2col :{opt mean}}means; the default{p_end}
{p2col :{opt median}}medians{p_end}
{p2col :{opt p1}}1st percentile{p_end}
{p2col :{opt p2}}2nd percentile{p_end}
{p2col :{it:...}}3rd{hline 1}49th percentiles{p_end}
{p2col :{opt p50}}50th percentile (same as {cmd:median}){p_end}
{p2col :{it:...}}51st{hline 1}97th percentiles{p_end}
{p2col :{opt p98}}98th percentile{p_end}
{p2col :{opt p99}}99th percentile{p_end}
{p2col :{opt sd}}standard deviations{p_end}
{p2col :{opt sem:ean}}standard error of the mean ({cmd:sd/sqrt(n)}){p_end}
{p2col :{opt seb:inomial}}standard error of the mean, binomial ({cmd:sqrt(p(1-p)/n)}){p_end}
{p2col :{opt sep:oisson}}standard error of the mean, Poisson ({cmd:sqrt(mean/n)}){p_end}
{p2col :{opt sum}}sums{p_end}
{p2col :{opt rawsum}}sums, ignoring an optionally specified weight except that
observations with a weight of zero are excluded{p_end}
{p2col :{opt count}}number of nonmissing observations{p_end}
{p2col :{opt percent}}percentage of nonmissing observations in the {cmd:by} group{p_end}
{p2col :{opt max}}maximums{p_end}
{p2col :{opt min}}minimums{p_end}
{p2col :{opt iqr}}interquartile range{p_end}
{p2col :{opt first}}first value{p_end}
{p2col :{opt last}}last value{p_end}
{p2col :{opt firstnm}}first nonmissing value{p_end}
{p2col :{opt lastnm}}last nonmissing value{p_end}
{p2colreset}{...}

{pstd}
If {it:stat} is not specified, {opt mean} is assumed.

{synoptset 20 tabbed}{...}
{synopthdr}
{synoptline}
{syntab:Options}
{synopt :{opth by(varlist)}}groups over which {it:stat} is to be calculated{p_end}
{synopt :{opt cw}}casewise deletion instead of all possible observations{p_end}
{synopt :{opt fast}}accepted for {cmd:collapse} compatibility; see {help ccollapse##fast:Options}{p_end}
{synopt :{opt clabel}}label the targets "{it:stat} of {it:varname}"{p_end}
{synopt :{opt frame(name)}}accepted for {cmd:collapse} compatibility; no effect{p_end}
{synoptline}
{syntab:Advanced}
{synopt :{opt thr:eads(#)}}maximum number of threads to use{p_end}
{synopt :{opt v:erbose}}display timing breakdown{p_end}
{synoptline}

{pstd}
{it:varlist} and {it:varname} in {it:clist} may contain time-series operators; see
{help tsvarlist}.{p_end}
{pstd}
{opt aweight}s, {opt fweight}s, {opt iweight}s, and {opt pweight}s are allowed;
see {help weight} and {help ccollapse##weights:Weights} below. {opt pweight}s may
not be used with {opt sd}, {opt semean}, {opt sebinomial}, or {opt sepoisson}.
{opt iweight}s may not be used with {opt semean}, {opt sebinomial}, or
{opt sepoisson}. {opt aweight}s may not be used with {opt sebinomial} or
{opt sepoisson}.


{marker description}{...}
{title:Description}

{* ctools-credit:begin (generated by scripts/sync_attribution.py; edit config/attribution.json)}{...}
{pstd}
{cmd:ccollapse} reimplements Stata's {cmd:collapse} ({cmd:collapse.ado} 6.2.2,
June 28, 2020). See {help ccollapse##acknowledgments:Acknowledgments} for
details.
{* ctools-credit:end}{...}

{pstd}
{cmd:ccollapse} converts the dataset in memory into a dataset of means, sums,
medians, and other summary statistics, one observation per {opt by()} group. It
takes the syntax of {helpb collapse} and produces the dataset {cmd:collapse}
produces: the same observations in the same order, and the same variable names,
storage types, display formats, variable labels and sort order. The statistics
are computed in parallel in a C plugin.

{pstd}
Every statistic is computed before the dataset is changed. The observations are
read once, in parallel; the groups are found without sorting the observations
whenever the {opt by()} values allow it (data already in order, whole numbers
in a small range, or few distinct values); and each group's sums are formed in
the order of its observations, so that results do not depend on the number of
threads.

{pstd}
If {it:clist} names a target twice, names a {opt by()} variable as a target, or
asks for a statistic of a string variable other than {opt first}, {opt last},
{opt firstnm} and {opt lastnm}, {cmd:ccollapse} stops with {cmd:collapse}'s
message and return code, and the data are left as they were.


{marker options}{...}
{title:Options}

{dlgtab:Options}

{phang}
{opth by(varlist)} specifies the groups over which the statistics are to be
calculated. Without it, the resulting dataset has one observation. The variables
may be numeric or string, and missing values form groups of their own. The
{opt by()} variables keep their storage type, format, labels, value labels,
characteristics and notes, and the result is sorted by them.

{phang}
{opt cw} specifies casewise deletion: observations with a missing value in any
of the source variables are left out of every statistic. Without {opt cw}, each
statistic uses all the observations in which its own source variable is not
missing.

{marker fast}{...}
{phang}
{opt fast} is {cmd:collapse}'s request not to restore the original dataset
should the user press {hi:Break}. {cmd:ccollapse} does not change the data until
every statistic has been computed, so the original data are in memory after
{hi:Break} or an error with or without {opt fast}, and no copy of the dataset is
made. The option is accepted and has no effect.

{phang}
{opt clabel}, an undocumented option of {cmd:collapse}, labels each target
"{it:stat} of {it:varname}" instead of "({it:stat}) {it:varname}".

{phang}
{opt frame(name)} is in the syntax of {cmd:collapse}, which does not use it.
{cmd:ccollapse} accepts it in the same way.

{dlgtab:Advanced}

{phang}
{opt threads(#)} sets the maximum number of threads, from 1 to 256. Zero uses the default.
The default follows the {help ctools##threads:ctools thread setting}.

{phang}
{opt verbose} displays the elapsed time of each step, the number of
observations and groups, and how the groups were found.


{marker remarks}{...}
{title:Remarks}

{pstd}
{ul:Storage types and labels.} As in {cmd:collapse}, {opt sum}, {opt rawsum}
and {opt percent} are stored as doubles; {opt count} as a long, or as a double
with {opt fweight}s, {opt iweight}s and {opt pweight}s; {opt max}, {opt min},
{opt first}, {opt last}, {opt firstnm} and {opt lastnm} in the storage type of
the source variable; and every other statistic as a double when the source is a
double or a long, and otherwise in the default type (see {helpb set type}).
Targets take the display format of their source and the label
"({it:stat}) {it:varname}"; percentiles are labelled "(p {it:#})".

{pstd}
{ul:Missing values.} A statistic of a group with no nonmissing value is missing,
except that {opt sum} and {opt rawsum} are 0 and {opt count} is 0. {opt min} and
{opt max} of such a group are its smallest and largest missing value;
{opt firstnm} and {opt lastnm} are its first and last value.

{marker weights}{...}
{pstd}
{ul:Weights.} Observations with a zero or missing weight are left out. With
{opt aweight}s, {opt count} is the number of observations and {cmd:ccollapse}
displays {cmd:collapse}'s note to that effect; with the other weights it is the
sum of the weights. {opt percent} uses the weights with every weight type.
Negative weights are an error except with {opt iweight}s, and {opt fweight}s
must be integers.

{pstd}
{ul:Agreement with collapse.} Counts, percentages, minimums, maximums, first and
last values, percentiles and the interquartile range are those of
{cmd:collapse} exactly, apart from weighted percentiles at an exact boundary
(below). Sums and means are identical when the data are sorted
by the {opt by()} variables, where {cmd:collapse} too adds each group's values
in the order of the observations. On data in another order {cmd:collapse}
arranges the observations of a group in an order of its own, which changes from
run to run, so its sums of numbers that are not exactly representable vary in
the last digits; {cmd:ccollapse} always adds in the order of the observations
and gives the same result each time. Stata/MP computes the sum of squared
deviations of a group in as many pieces as it has processors, so standard
deviations and standard errors of the mean can differ from {cmd:collapse}'s in
the last digit; with {cmd:set processors 1} they are identical for unweighted,
{opt fweight}ed and {opt iweight}ed data.

{pstd}
{ul:Weighted percentiles at an exact boundary.} When a weighted percentile
falls exactly between two values and the weights are not exactly representable
(0.1, 0.7, ...), {cmd:collapse}'s own result depends on the order of the
observations and on {cmd:set processors}: it adds the weights of parts of the
group whose make-up depends on that order, and the rounding of those sums
decides the boundary. {cmd:ccollapse} adds the weights of the sorted group from
the lowest value up, so its result does not change with the order or the
threads, and at such a boundary it can be the other of {cmd:collapse}'s two
answers. Weights whose sums are exact (integers, halves, {cmd:float} values)
give identical results. Groups whose {opt iweight}s add to zero or less follow
{cmd:collapse}'s rescaling of the weights to w*n/W; a total of exactly zero
gives missing.

{pstd}
{ul:Differences from collapse.} {opt sepoisson} is computed for groups whose
total reaches 2^31, where {cmd:collapse} stores a value that is not a number.
A result that overflows past Stata's largest double (about 8.99e307), such as
the {opt sum} or {opt iqr} of values near that limit, is stored as missing
({cmd:.}), where {cmd:collapse} stores the raw IEEE value, which Stata lists as
{cmd:.z_}. A {opt by()} variable may also be a source variable (under another target
name), which {cmd:collapse} refuses. {opt fast} has no effect, as described
above. The undocumented {opt graphbar()} option that {helpb graph bar} passes to
{cmd:collapse} is not available.

{pstd}
{ul:strL variables.} The plugin cannot write {cmd:strL} values, so when a
{opt by()} or source variable is a {cmd:strL}, {cmd:ccollapse} calls
{cmd:collapse}, and the result is {cmd:collapse}'s. The same holds under
{help version} 4 or earlier, where {cmd:collapse} runs its old command.


{marker examples}{...}
{title:Examples}

{pstd}Means of two variables, by group:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. sysuse auto, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. ccollapse price mpg, by(foreign)}{p_end}

{pstd}Several statistics, with target names:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. sysuse auto, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. ccollapse (mean) price (sd) sd_price=price (median) med_price=price (count) n=price, by(rep78)}{p_end}

{pstd}Percentiles and the interquartile range, with frequency weights:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. webuse census, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. ccollapse (p25) medage (p75) p75=medage (iqr) iqr=medage [fweight=pop], by(region)}{p_end}

{pstd}First and last values of a string variable:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. sysuse auto, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. ccollapse (first) make (last) last_make=make (max) price, by(foreign)}{p_end}

{pstd}Limit the threads and display the timing:{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. sysuse nlsw88, clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. ccollapse (mean) wage hours (p50) med_wage=wage, by(industry occupation) threads(4) verbose}{p_end}


{marker results}{...}
{title:Stored results}

{pstd}
{cmd:ccollapse} stores the following in global scalars (accessible via
{cmd:scalar(_ccollapse_...)}):

{synoptset 24 tabbed}{...}
{p2col 5 24 28 2: Scalars}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_ccollapse_nobs}}number of observations used{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_ccollapse_ngroups}}number of groups{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_ccollapse_threads}}number of threads used{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_ccollapse_method}}how the groups were found: 1 one group,
2 observations already in order, 3 whole-number keys in a small range,
4 hash table, 5 sort{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_ccollapse_time_load}}seconds reading the data{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_ccollapse_time_sample}}seconds applying weights and {opt cw}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_ccollapse_time_group}}seconds finding the groups{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_ccollapse_time_stats}}seconds computing the statistics{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_ccollapse_time_store}}seconds writing the collapsed dataset{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:_ccollapse_time_total}}seconds in the plugin before the dataset is replaced{p_end}

{pstd}
When {cmd:collapse} does the work ({cmd:strL} variables, old versions), the
scalars are not set.


